From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:30:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: Telegram calls through the Phone app, with
 tgcalls-lite and video

* Place and take calls through the Phone app over LS2

tdlib-purple already does the whole call - TDLib signalling and libtgvoip
media - but its UI is Pidgin's: a purple_request_action() accept dialog, a
"/hangup" command, and a gate on the front-end's PurpleMedia capabilities.
webOS has none of that. What it has is the Phone app, which drives calls
of other services through an LS2 contract, as it does for the WhatsApp
connector.

call-luna.{h,cpp} serves that contract as com.palm.telegram.call,
registered when the account connects, on libpurple's own GLib main loop:
callStateQuery (subscription), dial, answer, disconnect and hangupAll. They
reach TDLib through three bridge functions in call.cpp (callBridgeDial/
Answer/Hangup), and updateCall() pushes every state change - incoming,
dialing, active, disconnected with a cause (normal, rejected, missed,
error) - instead of writing to the conversation or opening a dialog. The
media-caps gate is bypassed. The accept/cancel dialog's callbacks
(acceptCallCb, discardCallCb) have no caller left and are removed;
answering goes through acceptCurrentCall().

Call audio: libtgvoip plays to the PulseAudio sink "voipcall" and records
from the source "webcall", the call devices module-palm-policy routes to
the earpiece, speaker or headset and the matching microphone. While the
call is active the bridge reports it to audiod
(com.palm.audio/phone/CallStatusUpdate, transport com.palm.telegram), and
clears it on hangup, from the service's own handle. No audio scenario is
forced, so a call starts on the earpiece (or a connected headset) and the
Phone app's speaker button moves it.

Dialing is made to cope with how the Phone app dials:

- an "id<n>" buddy whose user TDLib has not loaded yet (right after login)
  is called by id directly; createCall only needs the id.
- a bare "+E.164" number (dialpad, or a contact not linked to a Telegram
  buddy) is resolved with searchUserByPhoneNumber first, local contacts or
  not; if it is not on Telegram the call card is closed with an error
  instead of hanging on "Connecting".
- the dialer may send both the resolved "id<n>" and the raw number for one
  contact in the same tick. createCall is asynchronous, so hasActiveCall()
  is still false for the second; a synchronous "initiating" flag drops it,
  and its card is closed.
- an outgoing call is reported under the exact address that was dialed,
  so the plugin's card merges with the dialer's instead of leaving a
  second one stuck on "Connecting".

Calls are voice only: there is no video path for libtgvoip on LuneOS, so a
video request is placed as a voice call and video is never reported.

callProtocol.library_versions_ is left empty on purpose: declaring "2.7.7"
makes current clients move to a newer signalling path the bundled
VoIPController cannot speak, and audio breaks with it.

Building needs luna-service2 (lunaservice_INCLUDE_DIRS/_LIBRARIES).
-Dtgvoip_NO_DSP=ON matches a libtgvoip built with --disable-dsp. The call
life cycle goes to libpurple's debug log.

The tests have no bus, so they build test/call-luna-stub.cpp, whose
call service functions do nothing, in place of call-luna.cpp.

Not for upstream: the LS2 service, the Phone app contract and audiod are
webOS only.

* Carry calls with tgcalls-lite when the peer speaks tgcalls V2

Current Telegram clients run calls on tgcalls. The versions they register
are 2.7.7 and 5.0.0 (InstanceImpl) and 7.0.0 to 13.0.0 (InstanceV2Impl).
None of them is libtgvoip, so with library_versions left empty a call has
no engine both ends share.

tgcalls-lite (luneos-synergy-connectors, im/telegram/plugin/tgcalls-lite)
implements tgcalls V2 with signalling version 7.0.0:
- signalling packets encrypted as tgcalls' EncryptedConnection does and
  relayed by TDLib;
- ICE through libnice, using the STUN/TURN servers TDLib hands over;
- DTLS-SRTP and Opus;
- audio on the PulseAudio call devices libtgvoip already uses
  ("voipcall" and "webcall").

When it is built in (tgcalls_lite_INCLUDE_DIRS/_LIBRARIES):

- callProtocol.library_versions is ["7.0.0"];
- if the agreed protocol in callStateReady names 7.0.0, the call runs
  on tgcalls-lite. Its signalling goes out through sendCallSignalingData
  and comes in through updateNewCallSignalingData. Signalling that
  arrives before callStateReady is kept until the engine starts;
- otherwise the call stays on libtgvoip as before;
- LUNEOS_TELEGRAM_CALL_ENGINE=tgvoip in the service's environment turns
  tgcalls-lite off, for comparison.

Telegram's own reflectors (callServerTypeTelegramReflector) speak tgcalls'
ReflectorPort protocol, not TURN, so tgcalls-lite only uses the WebRTC
STUN/TURN servers. Calls are voice only: no video source or sink is wired
to the engine yet. The plug-in is built as C++17 when tgcalls-lite is
in, because its headers need it.

Also fixed: when a call could not be activated, the VoIPController was
deleted without Stop(), and libtgvoip aborts the process in that case.
It is now stopped first.

* Clear ended calls from the Phone app, show the peer's number

The Phone app removes a call card only when a callStateQuery push no
longer lists the call. com.palm.telegram.call kept reporting the ended
call as "disconnected" for good, so its "Ended" card stayed on screen
and kept the Phone app open after the next call. Push the empty line
list 1.5 s after "disconnected", as the WhatsApp connector does; any
newer push cancels it.

Incoming call cards showed the "id<n>" buddy name, so the Phone app
found no contact and offered to add one. When Telegram shares the
peer's phone number, the card shows it in E.164 form instead. An
outgoing call keeps the address that was dialled, which the Phone app
keyed its card to.

Every call went out as call id "tg". The Phone app remembers the ids of
the calls it has seen end and ignores a later "disconnected" for one of
them, so only the first call after the Phone app started closed its
card; every later one stayed up. Each call now gets an id of its own,
taken from the clock when it starts, so a restarted service does not
reuse one either. audiod still gets "tg": it keeps one call per sender.

* Video in tgcalls-lite calls through luneos-rtc-engine

Telegram calls carried by tgcalls-lite get video the way WhatsApp calls
do: org.webosports.rtcengine owns the camera, the H.264 encoder and
decoder and the shared-memory frames the Phone app shows; this side
decides when an engine session runs and moves access units between the
engine's two SOCK_SEQPACKET sockets and the call engine.

tgcalls-video.{h,cpp} hold that logic with no TDLib or luna-service2
dependency, so test/tgcalls-video-test.cpp exercises it on its own:
- AuBridge passes the peer's units to the engine without ever
  blocking libpurple's loop, holds them back until an IDR after a drop
  or a (re)connect, and reads our encoded camera on a thread of its own.
- Controller asks for capabilities, starts a session (with TX when our
  camera is wanted and the device can encode, receive-only when the user
  only watches the peer), restarts it when the camera is toggled, stops
  it when the call ends, ignores replies to sessions it already closed,
  and passes the peer's keyframe requests to the encoder at most once a
  second.

The Phone app talks to com.palm.telegram.call as it does to the
WhatsApp connector: dial takes "video", the new changeMedia method takes
incomingVideo/outgoingVideo, and the call state carries incomingVideo,
outgoingVideo, incomingVideoState, incomingVideoOrientation and the
frame/preview paths. A wish made before the engine runs is kept and
applied when it does. audiod is told the call carries video.

Our picture is sent rotated by 90 degrees (the CVO extension); that
value has not been checked against a live call yet.

* Log why TDLib ended a call

When a Telegram call is answered on another device, sargo showed it
as missed. TDLib's log is capped at warnings (0009), so nothing
recorded which discard reason the server sent. Log one line per
ending call update: the state, TDLib's discard reason, whether the
call is outgoing and whether it is the one the Phone app shows. No
peer data is logged.

* A Telegram call answered on another device is not missed

When another device of the same account takes an incoming call, the
server discards it here with no reason (callDiscardReasonEmpty); a
caller who gives up while it rings gives callDiscardReasonMissed.
Both were pushed to the Phone app as an end it had not acted on, so
it showed a missed call.

Tell the Phone app "answeredElsewhere" for an incoming call that was
not answered here (no accept sent, never ready) and ended with no
reason. Declined and missed keep their causes; anything else stays
"normal". The rule lives in call-end.h with a standalone host test.

Seen on sargo: answered on another device gave reason=empty, a call
the caller cancelled gave reason=missed (logged by 0019).

* Our picture upright on the far side, and turning with the phone

A tgcalls receiver needs our camera's picture turned 270 degrees for
sargo held upright (90 arrived upside down on Android), and the
Phone app's own inset keeps its one quarter turn: the two are separate
values now. The rotation follows the phone: the Phone app sends
changeMedia {deviceOrientation} (the shell's quarter turns) while our
camera is sent, and tgvideo::txRotationDegrees(camera, turns) gives
270 less a quarter per turn for the front camera, more for the back
one; the engine sends it in MediaState (and CVO) as the phone turns.
test/video-rotation-test.cpp covers the arithmetic. Checked on sargo
against Android, both ways sideways.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
